% pmodd: perfect matching on an odd number of vertices.  Choose
% edges of the complete graph K_{2N+1} so that every vertex is an
% endpoint of exactly one chosen edge -- impossible, the vertex
% count is odd.  One 0/1 var per edge; for each vertex the equation
% sum of its incident vars = 1.  UNSAT for every N.  The counting
% argument needs the DIVIDE rule of cutting planes: every edge has
% two endpoints, so summing the vertex equations gives 2*sum(x) =
% 2N+1; dividing the two halves and rounding apart gives sum(x) >=
% N+1 and sum(x) <= N.  The LP relaxation is feasible (x_e = 1/2N),
% so an LP solver alone cannot refute the instance, and resolution
% proofs are exponential (Razborov 2004) -- the divide-by-2 subclass
% of the pb advantage.  Caveat: the built-in sat and cp paths
% answer in ms too (their lowering of the equations carries the
% counting in auxiliary structure or in propagation), so the
% exponential separation shows against direct clausal encodings,
% not against the built-in solvers.
% Sized by the command line:
%   picat pmodd.pi <n>       (vertices = 2n + 1)
% The same program body runs unchanged under import pb, import cp
% and import sat; only the import line decides the solver.  pb sends
% the linear form to an external pseudo-Boolean solver (env PBSOL);
% cp and sat are built in.  No timing here -- the sweep script
% test/pb_families_bench.sh measures all three solvers.
%
% With pb:  PBSOL=<solver> PICATPATH=<picat>/lib2 emu/picat pmodd.pi 7
% With cp:  emu/picat pmodd.pi 7              (after the import swap)

import pb, os.

main([NS]) =>
    N = NS.to_int,
    Vn = 2 * N + 1,
    E = N * Vn,                     % Vn*(Vn-1)/2 edges
    X = new_array(E),
    X :: 0..1,
    foreach (W in 1..Vn)
        sum([X[K] : K in inc(W, Vn)]) #= 1
    end,
    bp.c_wall_ms(T0),
    ( solve(X) ->
        bp.c_wall_ms(T1),
        printf("SAT: a perfect matching exists on %d vertices (%d ms)%n", Vn, T1 - T0)
    ;
        bp.c_wall_ms(T1),
        printf("UNSAT: no perfect matching on %d vertices (%d ms)%n", Vn, T1 - T0)
    ).

% dense edge numbering: edge(U,V), U < V, gets
% (U-1)*Vn - (U-1)*U/2 + (V-U), in 1..Vn*(Vn-1)/2
edge(U, V, Vn) = (U - 1) * Vn - (U - 1) * U // 2 + (V - U).

% the edges incident to vertex W
inc(W, Vn) = [edge(W, V, Vn) : V in W + 1..Vn] ++ [edge(U, W, Vn) : U in 1..W - 1].
